  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1151 AM CST Wed Nov 4 2026\n\nOccasional rain continues through Thursday\n\nUpper low spinning over the Twin Cities as of 17z will slowly churn \neastward today, become an open wave by tomorrow with the resulting \ntrough sliding southeastward around midday Thursday before a \nnorthern stream wave currently over far northern Manitoba dive \nsoutheast over Wisconsin. As evidenced by widespread cloudiness and \n04.12z RAOBs, moisture across the region is more than enough for \nlight rain to develop as occasional forcing for ascent develops \nahead of shortwaves rotating around the upper low and should remain \nso as the main body of the upper trough moves southeast tomorrow. \nGiven the relatively weak forcing, am not concerned about hazardous \nrainfall amounts. Indeed, maximum values in the 04.12z REFS are \n0.75\" or lower and 75th percentile amounts are under 1/2\". While the \nnorthern Manitoba wave arriving Thursday is much stronger, this \nlooks to largely miss our CWA to the east so a few extra showers \nclipping Clark/Taylor/Juneau/Adams would be the main concern later \ntomorrow.\n\nTurning drier and cooler Friday, slow rebound in temps into Monday\n\nLarge scale descent in the wake of the upper wave Thursday will lead \nto clearing skies and cooler temperatures for Friday. Friday night, \nentire region almost certainly (99%) will drop below freezing into \nSaturday morning. Users should note that, since the growing season \nhas already ended, no Freeze Watches, Frost Advisories, or Freeze \nWarnings will be issued until spring.\n\nAfter a northern and southern stream wave pass well to our northeast \nand southwest, respectively, on Sunday, heights aloft rise into the \nnew work week next Monday. Temperatures will once again rise to \nabove normal for this time of year, potentially (40-90% per 04.13z \nNBM) above 60.",
